; 15-expect.kurt

; So far, whenever a lesson wanted to say "this would fail", it could only
; describe it in a comment and ask you to try it yourself (see 05-const.kurt,
; 06-var.kurt).  `expect` lets a file demonstrate a mistake directly, and
; have Kurt itself confirm it still fails the way the lesson says it does:
; `expect "KIND"` opens a block whose content must raise an error of the
; given kind (one of `ProofError`, `ParseError`, `EvalError`, `SyntaxError`,
; `TypeError`) -- if it does, the block (and everything inside it) is simply
; discarded and checking continues from the next line; if it doesn't, *that*
; is now the failure.

const zero

expect "EvalError"
    var zero          ; `zero` is already a constant -- this really does fail

true                  ; ...and we're back to normal, checking continues here

; `expect` only checks the *kind* of error, not its exact wording -- so a
; lesson (or a test) using it doesn't quietly go stale the moment an error
; message's phrasing changes, the way copying the exact text into a comment
; (or into a test's expected output) would.

; One limitation, for now: the block inside `expect` may not itself open
; another block (`show`/`proof`/`assume`/`case`/`let`/`pick`/`sandbox`) --
; if it needs to, the failure surfaces from that inner block instead, and
; `expect` won't catch it there; the file just fails normally, exactly as
; if `expect` had not been used at all.
